use "scoring"
use util as text

// BIG-F12 application experiment.
// Behavior oracle to verify: score 91 plus offset 0 is "A"; a successful
// executable run prints exactly `A` and then `hello!`. The hidden and exposed
// paths must agree.
// Module/dependent inventory: scoring.jet owns the hidden decision and its
// nominal Score/Offset contract; this file is its consumer. util.jet is the
// exposed-Int counterpart and the unrelated shout consumer.
// A representation-only edit to scoring.jet changes no caller here: callers
// use its constructors and `letter`, not its private fields. There is no
// runtime or compile-time cost claim until matched binaries are measured.
fn run() {
    score :: scoring.score(91)
    offset :: scoring.offset(0)
    hidden :: scoring.letter(score, offset)
    exposed :: text.exposed_letter(91, 0)
    if hidden != exposed -> panic("hidden and exposed scoring disagree")
    print(hidden)
    print(text.shout("hello"))
}

// Deliberate role-confusion witness (kept out of the executable):
// `scoring.letter(offset, score)` swaps two nominal roles and should be
// rejected by the current checker. `text.exposed_letter(0, 91)` still
// type-checks because both roles are undifferentiated Int. This oracle uses
// addition, so swapping happens to preserve its number; the contrast is that
// only the nominal contract can reject the misuse before runtime. The
// JIT and AOT runs of this executable module body printed A then hello!;
// the reversed nominal call is E0112, while the reversed Int call type-checks.
// A scratch-only representation change added a private field in scoring.jet
// without changing run.jet or util.jet. Timings are coarse observations, not
// a zero-overhead or performance claim.
